course: dictionary
lesson: 12859
title: Engrossing: lost in a good story
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some books and films are so interesting that they hold all your attention. You forget the time, the clock and the people around you. That is what engrossing means. «The engrossing book kept Mia reading until midnight.» Mia did not stop because the book was so interesting that it took all her attention.
explain: Engrossing is an adjective, so it describes a noun: an engrossing story, an engrossing game. Its verb is engross, and the -ing form shows the same idea: something that takes up someone's whole attention. «Ben was engrossed in his puzzle and did not hear the bell.» Engrossed means so busy with the puzzle that Ben noticed nothing else.
explain: Being engrossed is great for a good book, but it can be risky in other places. A child who is engrossed in a phone while crossing a road may not notice the cars coming. Good readers enjoy being engrossed, and they also keep their eyes up when they walk near traffic.
fact: Engrossing means so interesting that it holds all your attention.
fact: «An engrossing story» is one you cannot put down.
fact: Engross is the verb: it means to take up someone's whole attention.
fact: If you are engrossed in something, you do not notice what is happening around you.
fact: Being engrossed in a phone while crossing a road can be dangerous, because you may not see or hear the traffic.
exercise: «The engrossing book kept Mia reading until midnight.» What does engrossing mean here? | Boring and too long | Very heavy and hard to lift | *So interesting that it holds all your attention | Written in a foreign language
exercise: «Ben was engrossed in his puzzle and did not hear the bell.» What does engrossed mean? | *So busy with it that he noticed nothing else | Angry with the puzzle | Bored and tired of the puzzle | Finished with the puzzle long ago
exercise: Find and copy the word in «The film was so engrossing that nobody moved» that tells you the film held everyone's attention. | moved | *engrossing | film | so
test: Which word best describes a book you cannot put down? | Noisy | *Engrossing | Tiny | Dull
test: Why can being engrossed in a phone be risky when you cross a road? | Phones are too heavy to carry | *You may not notice the traffic around you | Roads are engrossing to look at | You cannot see your phone clearly
exam: Which sentence uses engrossing correctly? | My shoes are very engrossing today | The soup was engrossing because it was hot | *The mystery novel was so engrossing that I read it all night | The rain was engrossing on the window
